NAME: trawl
LAST DEPLOYED: Mon Sep 28 11:41:23 2026
NAMESPACE: trawl-cm
STATUS: deployed
REVISION: 1
DESCRIPTION: Install complete
NOTES:
Trawl has been installed.

Create roles and API keys against the Fleet database:
  https://trawl.sh/operate/access/#create-roles-and-keys
Use a personal API key to sign in to the browser and a service key for each collector.
The init-auth container initializes the database schema; it does not create API keys.

The chart requested Certificate trawl-tls in namespace trawl-cm.
It uses Issuer trawl-ca-issuer
in API group cert-manager.io. cert-manager and this issuer must already exist.
The Issuer must be in namespace trawl-cm.
cert-manager fills Secret trawl-tls; the daemon mounts tls.crt and tls.key from it.
Wait for issuance before waiting for the StatefulSet:

  kubectl wait --namespace trawl-cm certificate/trawl-tls --for=condition=Ready --timeout=120s

If issuance fails, inspect the Certificate and its issuer; the chart does not install cert-manager or create issuers.
Requested API certificate DNS names:
  trawl.trawl-cm.svc.cluster.local
  trawl.cm.example.test
Remote API clients must use a covered DNS name and trust the issuer's CA.

Browser-ingress TLS is configured separately through ingress.tls.

Check the HTTPS API through a local port-forward:

  kubectl port-forward --namespace trawl-cm svc/trawl 5514:5514

Keep the port-forward running. In another terminal on this machine:

  curl --fail-with-body --insecure https://localhost:5514/api/v1/health

Expect status "ok" and every entry in "checks" to be "ok". HTTP 200 alone can
report degraded dependencies. --insecure is only for this local check.

Configured browser origins:
  http://localhost:8090

For the configured local browser origin, keep this port-forward running:

  kubectl port-forward --namespace trawl-cm svc/trawl 8090:8090

Open http://localhost:8090 and sign in with your personal API key.

